{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ europe european eu) (+ russia russian) gas (+ lng storage imports))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_18_1770243199#1_1996337687","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q224","rank":1,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_18_1770243199#1_1996337687\" , \"end_char\": 1918.000000 , \"headings\": \"Russia in the European energy sector Russia in the European energy sector Contents History Natural gas deliveries Disputes and diversification efforts Nuclear fuel supplies See also References \" , \"segment\": \"In 2017, energy products accounted around 60% of the EU's total imports from Russia. According to Eurostat, 30% of the <cover>EU's petroleum oil imports and 39% of total gas imports came from Russia </cover>in 2017. For Estonia, Poland, Slovakia and Finland, more than 75% of their imports of petroleum oils originated in Russia. The Russian state-owned company Gazprom exports natural gas to Europe. It also controls many subsidiaries, including various infrastructure assets. According to a study published by the Research Centre for East European Studies, the liberalization of the EU gas market has driven Gazprom's expansion in Europe by increasing its share in the European downstream market. It has established sale subsidiaries in many of its export markets, and has also invested in access to industrial and power generation sectors in "}}
